  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are moving through this morning as the \nfront has stalled out across the southern half of the state. This \nconvection has expanded as the LLJ increased, currently over the \nsouthern half of IL. The heaviest rain should remain along and south \nof the I-70 corridor. There is a low risk for flash flooding across \nthe southeast. This area can likely take quite a bit of rain before \nflooding becomes a major concern. The 6 hour FFG for the southeast \nis 3-5 inches and 3 hr FFG is 3-4 inches. PWATs in the area of the \nstorms is 1.9-2.2 inches. Localized swaths of 1 to 3 inches are \nstill possible (particularly south of I-70) as highlighted by the \nHREF LPMM. WPC has maintained a marginal risk ERO along and south of \nI-72. The activity should move out by early afternoon.\n\nHigh pressure will build over the Great Lakes Friday. Then \nadditional low-end chances exist for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the weekend. The NBM has broad brushed 30-50% POPs through \nmuch of next week starting Tuesday as a cold front looks to be \nprogged to move through Illinois and potentially stall out. \n\nMore seasonable temperatures will be in place today and tomorrow \nwith warming temperatures through the weekend. Mid to late week next \nweek is forecast to be near normal behind the cold frontal passage \nTuesday. The Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 6-10 day outlook \nindicates a 40-70% probability of above-normal temperatures \nacross central Illinois through September 19th. Normal highs \nthis time of year is in the low 80s and normal lows in the upper\n50s.",
